```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is not a cabling problem. Every physical interface across all five
routers is administratively and operationally UP (LOWER_UP), so no fabric
cable needs to be reseated.

The real fault is that the bgpd daemon has been stopped or killed on
leaf_router_0_0 and has not been restarted. With no BGP process running, the
router cannot establish eBGP sessions with its two uplinks
(spine_router_0_0 via 172.16.0.4 and spine_router_0_1 via 172.16.0.8). As a
result, leaf_router_0_0 holds only its three directly connected prefixes and
never learns 10.0.1.0/24 from the fabric. Symmetrically, neither spine nor
super_spine ever receive a BGP advertisement for 10.0.0.0/24, so the entire
upstream fabric is blind to pc_0_0's subnet. When pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) tries to
reach pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2), leaf_router_0_0 responds immediately with ICMP
Destination Net Unreachable — explaining the 100% packet-loss symptom.

The configuration file /etc/frr/frr.conf on leaf_router_0_0 is intact and
correct (AS 65200, router-id 172.16.0.5, network 10.0.0.0/24, both spine
neighbors). Nothing needs to be edited.

Root cause: the bgpd process on leaf_router_0_0 is not running.

Corrective fix: on leaf_router_0_0 run `systemctl restart frr` (or
`service frr restart`). This will reload the existing correct frr.conf, start
bgpd, bring up both eBGP sessions to the spines, and propagate 10.0.0.0/24
throughout the fabric within seconds, restoring full pc_0_0 ↔ pc_0_1
reachability.
=== END ===
```